{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 121 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b89c886dc3e4eabea5e44a02d9c8cf23d1ce5e31e44ac06bed86e605db2adc7e" :edb/db-before-t 120 :edb/db-after-t 121 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533433 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:55.816Z" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186059764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360774 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186059815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360683 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059286 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360420 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060449 1071 "$\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060449 1071 "What is $\\frac{3\\textrm{i}}{1 + \\textrm{i}}$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060449 1074 "The denominator is $1+\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059292 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360453 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060450 1071 "If $z_{1} = 2 - 5\\textrm{i}$ and $z_{2} = 6 + 3\\textrm{i}$, then $z_{1}$ divided by $z_{2}$ equals" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060450 1071 "If ${z}_{1}=2-5\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=6+3\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060450 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $6+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $6-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $6-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{6-3\\text{i}}{6-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6\\text{i}-30\\text{i}+15{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{6}^{2}-{3}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-36\\text{i}+15(-1)}{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-36\\text{i}-15}{36+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-36\\text{i}}{45}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3-36\\text{i}}{45}=-\\frac{3}{45}-\\frac{36}{45}\\text{i}=-\\frac{1}{15}-\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059310 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360198 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060453 1071 "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060453 1071 "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k + 1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060453 1074 "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2k+1 & k \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}2k+1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3-0)-(2k+1-k)-4(0-3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(k+1)-4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-k-1+12 \\\\  & =17-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17-k & =0 \\\\ k & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k=17.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059328 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360027 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060456 1071 "The entry $a_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $18$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186060456 1071 "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $18.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186060456 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-24)-2(0-4k)+3(0-k) \\\\  & =8k-3k-24 \\\\  & =5k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-18) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5k-24}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5k-24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{5k-24} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5k-24} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =5k-24 \\\\ 25 & =5k \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360656 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360657 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360658 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360659 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360660 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360661 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360662 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360663 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360664 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360665 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360666 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360667 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360668 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360669 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360670 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360671 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360672 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360673 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360674 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360675 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360676 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063863 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360291 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064973 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\pi \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064973 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\intπ\\sinh xdx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064973 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\pi \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\pi \\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063875 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360384 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064975 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int 2\\pi {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064975 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int2πsech^{2} xdx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064975 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={sech}^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\pi {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi \\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063881 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360156 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064976 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064976 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\sqrt{2}csch x\\coth xdx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064976 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063899 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360421 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064979 1071 "$\\int (5{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186064979 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int(5csch^{2} x - sech x\\tanh x)dx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186064979 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{csch}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360547 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360586 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360830 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082769 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186083978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360437 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083978 1071 "The heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{m}.$ Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10\\,\\text{m}$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186083978 1071 "The heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9m$ and a standard deviation of $4m$. Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10m$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.1$ | $0.5557$ | $0.5596$ | $0.5636$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.5948$ | $0.5987$ | $0.6026$ |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6331$ | $0.6368$ | $0.6406$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186083978 1074 "Let $X$ represent the heights (in meters) of the trees in the park. Then we have $X∼N(9,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<10).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=P(X\\le 10)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.25) \\\\  & =Φ(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.25)=0.5987≈0.60.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of a randomly selected tree being shorter than $10\\,\\text{m}$ is approximately $0.60.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360461 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360476 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360478 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360738 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360739 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186087760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360752 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087389 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186088461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360333 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088461 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $((x + 4)^{2})/(3) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(6) = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186088461 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088461 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087401 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186088463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360223 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088463 1071 "Determine the point where the ellipse $x^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4\\sqrt{3}x - 6\\sqrt{5}y + 15 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186088463 1071 "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186088463 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}(0)-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{5}y]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-5]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-15+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n![](images/d3/d37e546bf2b17c99c87650e7c0973a3a881c8c5f3a8ed28f5f923d9d3d500511.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360685 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360686 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360611 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360612 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360613 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360608 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360609 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360812 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091738 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186092928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360201 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092928 1071 "![](images/08/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $\\frac{π}{2} \\le θ \\le \\frac{5π}{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186092928 1071 "![](images/08/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092928 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091828 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186092943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360075 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092943 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y+3z+x=-1 \\\\ 4z+y=1 \\\\ 5x+6y-3=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186092943 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n${2y + 3z + x =-1; 4z + y = 1; 5x + 6y - 3 =-3$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186092943 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ y+4z=1 \\\\ 5x+6y=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-24)-2(-20)+3(-5) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 & -20 & -5 \\\\ -18 & -15 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 20 & -5 \\\\ 18 & -15 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=42,y=-35,z=9.$" 13194139533433 