\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124058 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360426 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125248 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0, ∞)$ to $R$ of the form $f(x) = a\\log_{2} (x)$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n2. For any $α, β ∈ R$ and $f(x) ∈ L$, we have $(α + β)f(x) = αf(x) + βf(x)$.\n3. For any $α ∈ R$ and any $f(x), g(x) ∈ L$, we have $α(f(x) + g(x)) = αf(x) + αg(x)$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125248 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125248 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124064 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360380 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125249 1071 "Given that $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2yz$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂θ}$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ, z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125249 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125249 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our 