c{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124094 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360310 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125254 1071 "Given that $f(x, y, z) = xy + y^{2}z + xz^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ, z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186125254 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy+{y}^{2}z+x{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186125254 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\