C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5⟨a,c⟩-2⟨b,c⟩ & =5\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-2⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-2⟨b,c⟩ & =5(\\overset{―}{-1+\\text{i}})-2(3-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =5(-1-\\text{i})-2(3-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-5-5\\text{i}-6+4\\text{i} \\\\  & =-11-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133350 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360163 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134557 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ over $C$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨a, b⟩ = ⟨b, a⟩^(―)$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$\n2. $⟨a, αb⟩ = α⟨a, b⟩$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$ and any $α ∈ C$\n3. $⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134557 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134557 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133356 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360101 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134558 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ over $C$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨a, 0⟩ = 0$ for any $a ∈ C^{n}$\n2. $2⟨a, b⟩ = ⟨a,-2b⟩$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$\n3. $⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134558 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,0⟩=0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $2⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-2b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134558 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the antilinearity in the second argument we have that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,0⟩ & =⟨a,0\\cdot 0⟩=\\overset{―}{0}⟨a,0⟩=0⟨a,0⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,-2b⟩=\\overset{―}{(-2)}⟨a,b⟩=-2⟨a,b⟩\\ne 2⟨a,b⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number, in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133362 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360449 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134559 1071 "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 5x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134559 1071 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134559 1074 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+5\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (k\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =-15-75+10k \\\\  & =-90+10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -90+10k & =0 \\\\ 10k & =90 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133368 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360036 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134560 1071 "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(0)y(0) + x(-1)y(-1)$.\nIf $p(t) = kt^{2} - 2t$ and $q(t) = 2t + 1$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134560 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k{t}^{2}-2t$ and $q(t)=2t+1$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134560 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(1)q(1)+p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(k-2)(2+1)+(0)(0+1)+(k+2)(-2+1) \\\\  & =(3k-6)+0+(-k-2) \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133374 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360081 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134561 1071 "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(0)y(0) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 4 - 2t + t^{2}$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134561 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=4-2t+{t}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134561 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=4-2t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(0)=4,\\,p(2)=4,\\,p(3)=7.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(0)p(0)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(0)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133386 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360072 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134563 1071 "Let $V = C[0, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}f(t)g(t)dt$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t) = \\cos (3t)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 0$\n2. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 2π$\n3. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\cos α\\cos β = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β))$.*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186134563 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186134563 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360689 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147050 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360330 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148221 1071 "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148221 1071 "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - 4$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148221 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nIn this case, we have \n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & >0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/bb/bbb6defd12fdc683f2df111cce7e10807fd8d69877ddf12de7fa97d3e9d53781.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147056 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360303 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148222 1071 "![](images/d3/d3a06bdc7a63a86651752ff70331010920d24728e7f4e4ad9f067eb860ebaf4b.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-1$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148222 1071 "![](images/d3/d3a06bdc7a63a86651752ff70331010920d24728e7f4e4ad9f067eb860ebaf4b.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n1. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - 1$\n2. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}(y + 1)$\n3. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148222 1074 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}-1,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,1),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147092 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360046 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148228 1071 "![](images/92/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a sink\n- $y=2$ is a node" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186148228 1071 "![](images/92/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y =-1$ is a source\n2. $y = 2$ is a sink\n3. $y = 2$ is a node" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186148228 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-1.$ This means that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=2.$This means that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360593 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360594 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360595 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360596 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360597 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360606 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360590 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360591 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360592 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360536 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360537 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360555 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360565 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162013 1038 #edb