 [17592186162648 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $2x_{1}^{2} - 6x_{1}x_{2} + 10x_{2}^{2} = 44$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186162648 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}=44?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162648 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +11 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =11,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =44 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{44} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161348 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186162681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360316 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162681 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/7c/7ccbc3dcc39f79dab841539230fc82112384027013a719ea02350174ec566d78.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161354 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186162682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360253 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162682 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $AA^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n2. The sum of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186162682 1071 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186162682 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360693 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360557 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360558 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360559 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360560 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360561 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360562 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360563 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360564 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360572 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360574 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360575 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360576 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360577 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360489 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360490 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360513 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360514 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360515 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360516 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360517 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360543 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166758 1038 #edb/ref 1759218