045585 true] [17592186166819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360697 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360698 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360699 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360704 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360705 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360706 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360743 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360744 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360687 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360688 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360694 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360695 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360696 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360729 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360730 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360731 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360763 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360764 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360766 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360769 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360770 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360732 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360733 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360734 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360735 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360824 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360825 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360826 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360832 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360833 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186166894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360834 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165177 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360044 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167333 1071 "If $f(x, y) = \\sin (x^{2} + y)$ and $y = x^{2}$, compute $\\frac{df}{dx}$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167333 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167333 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165189 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360372 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167335 1071 "Let $f(x, y, z) = 4x + 3y - 2z$, where $x = 2s^{2} - t$, $y = s + 3t^{2}$ and $z = 4s^{2} + t^{2}$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167335 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=4x+3y-2z,$ where $x=2{s}^{2}-t,$ $y=s+3{t}^{2}$ and $z=4{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167335 1074 "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =8s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(4)\\cdot 4s+(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 8s \\\\  & =16s+3-16s \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165201 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360301 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167337 1071 "If $f(x, y) = \\ln (xy^{2}), x = \\cos t$ and $y = \\sin t$, compute $\\frac{df}{dt}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167337 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167337 1074 "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165207 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360009 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167338 1071 "If $f(x, y) = x\\ln (x + y)$ and $y = x^{2}$, compute $\\frac{df}{dx}$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167338 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167338 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165243 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360374 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167344 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167344 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}}dx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167344 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=9\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=9\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165249 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360111 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167345 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167345 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{π}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}}dx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167345 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi \\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165255 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360398 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167346 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167346 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(\\sqrt{2})/(3(1 - x^{2}))dx$ for $\\mid x \\mid < 1$." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167346 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165356 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360338 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167363 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167363 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16x^{2} - 1}}dx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167363 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165548 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360458 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167395 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167395 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - y^{2} = 2y + y^{3}$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y = 0$\n2. $y = 1$\n3. $y = 2$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167395 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165584 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360411 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167401 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int (\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167401 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}} + \\sinh x)dx$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167401 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165608 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360058 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167405 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$\n- ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167405 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{3} - 7y + 6$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = 1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and $y_{3}(x)$\n3. $y_{1}(x) + y_{2}(x) + y_{3}(x) = 0$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167405 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165848 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360400 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167445 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + x^{3}y = 5y^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167445 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{3}y=5{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167445 1074 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ which has order $2.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165854 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360135 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167446 1071 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$\n- $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2xy=2{y}^{′}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-2x={y}^{″}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167446 1071 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $y^{‴} + 3xy^{′} = xy$\n2. $3y^{″}y^{′} - 2xy = 2y^{′}$\n3. $x^{3}y^{′} - 2x = y^{″}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167446 1074 "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165860 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360341 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167447 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167447 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{y}$\n2. $\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 0$\n3. $2\\frac{dy}{dx} - xy = \\sqrt{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167447 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165866 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360451 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167448 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167448 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $2xy^{′} = y, y(0) = 1$\n2. $y^{′} - 2xy = e^{y}$\n3. $2xy^{″} = y^{′}, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 1$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167448 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165914 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360082 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167456 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}+2x?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167456 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{2} + 2x$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167456 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(-2,2),$ and $(2,3).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=(2)^{2}+2(2)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/4b/4b6ef4f2b56acf2d09a07d73c19f49f5b1c6b9eee17caa382a5c9924cbb0ea5f.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165938 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360102 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167460 1071 "![](images/76/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167460 1071 "![](images/76/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x - 2$\n2. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)^{2}$\n3. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167460 1074 "![](images/11/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165944 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360290 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167461 1074 "![](images/df/dfd308fd43e27e7adaab6d40411cf33e53f0186faf5719c1ccbe9d934f552cca.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-1)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-1)(x+2).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165962 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360125 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167464 1071 "![](images/68/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167464 1071 "![](images/68/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{5x}{x - 2}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{x - 2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167464 1074 "![](images/74/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166016 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360373 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167473 1071 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167473 1071 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $y^{′} = e^{x}$\n2. $y^{′} - \\frac{3}{y} = 0$\n3. $\\frac{1}{2}y^{′} - xy = e^{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167473 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166088 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360028 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167485 1071 "Given that $X∼B(4, 0.2)$, compute $P(X = 3)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167485 1071 "Given that $X∼B(4,0.2),$ compute $P(X=3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167485 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{4-3} \\\\  & =4(0.2)^{3}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166124 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360025 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167491 1071 "Approximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\le z) = 0.1700$ using interval bisection.\n\n| $z$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1.0$ | $0.1469$ | $0.1446$ | $0.1423$ |\n| $-0.9$ | $0.1711$ | $0.1685$ | $0.1660$ |\n| $-0.8$ | $0.1977$ | $0.1949$ | $0.1922$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167491 1071 "Approximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.1700$ using interval bisection.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ | $\\,0.1446\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1711\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1977\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167491 1074 "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.1700$ lies between. These numbers are $0.1685$ and $0.1711.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1685 & <0.1700<0.1711\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $Φ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.96) & <Φ(z)<Φ(-0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz≈\\frac{-0.96+(-0.95)}{2}=-0.955.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166190 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360152 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167502 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167502 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{5}⟨ - 3\\sin t, 3\\cos t, 4⟩,; N(t)|= ⟨ - \\cos t,-\\sin t, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167502 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),-(0-(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166196 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360080 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167503 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t = 2$, given that\n\n$T(2)|= \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1, 2⟩,; N(2)|= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 1, 2, 2⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167503 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167503 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨(-2-4),-(4+2),(4-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨-6,-6,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166202 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360352 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167504 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167504 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\sin t, \\cos t⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167504 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166208 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360385 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167505 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167505 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{″} = 3y^{′}, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) = 1$\n2. $2x^{2}y^{′} = y, y(0) = 2$\n3. $4y^{′} - \\frac{1}{3}xy = \\ln y$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167505 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166274 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360353 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167516 1071 "Given $X∼B(6, 0.2)$, compute $P(3 \\le X \\le 5)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186167516 1071 "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186167516 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360709 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360710 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360711 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360712 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360713 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360714 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360716 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360717 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360718 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360719 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360720 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360721 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360722 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360723 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172040 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360725 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360785 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360786 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360787 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360788 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172045 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360827 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172045 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360828 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172045 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360829 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172047 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360831 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171405 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360014 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172635 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $f: x ↦ x^{2} - 3$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172635 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $f:x↦{x}^{2}-3?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172635 1074 "The notation $f:x↦{x}^{2}-3$ means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to ${x}^{2}-3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-3.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171411 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360056 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172636 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x) = 5x + x^{2}$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172636 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172636 1074 "The notation $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $5x+{x}^{2}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦5x+{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171429 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360261 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172639 1071 "![](images/dc/dcee145044fe79dc7f4d07c3c4c1248df358cd8eda7a173cd8d4641f13404122.png)\n\nConsider the function $f: X → Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The domain of $f$ is ${0, 1, 2, 3}$\n2. The codomain of $f$ is ${0,-1}$\n3. The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172639 1071 "![](images/dc/dcee145044fe79dc7f4d07c3c4c1248df358cd8eda7a173cd8d4641f13404122.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${0,1,2,3}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${0,-1}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172639 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={0,1,2,3},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={0,-1,-2},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${0,-1},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171435 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360326 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172640 1071 "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172640 1071 "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172640 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/b3/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](images/77/7715c9573d2a434bb5878ff3900632dae7121bd752bab6a9a7745f69de84f0ce.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171471 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360200 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172646 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172646 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = xy$?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172646 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/a3/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/9d/9d60c65cda12bb6ac800906e7889a8ecc395974dff0379130d647810c1623869.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171495 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360392 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172650 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the interval from $-1$ to $1$ exclusive to the set $Y.$\"*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172650 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n> *\"The function $f$ maps the interval from $-1$ to $1$ exclusive to the set $Y$.\"*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172650 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the interval from $-1$ to $1$ exclusive to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:(-1,1)\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $(-1,1),$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171501 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360318 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172651 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186172651 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n3. At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186172651 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186176095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360757 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360538 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360511 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360512 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360684 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183907 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186184888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360229 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184888 1071 "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ about $x = 9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}$. Give your answer to $5$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186184888 1071 "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186184888 1074 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186198360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360540 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360502 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360503 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360030 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234495 1071 "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}={x}^{2}y$\n- $5{y}^{″}{y}^{′}+3xy=4{y}^{′}$\n- ${({y}^{′})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234495 1071 "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}x){y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{y}^{''}{y}^{'}+3xy=4{y}^{'}$\nIII. - ${({y}^{'})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234495 1074 "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234495 1074 "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360029 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234500 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234500 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\nII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}y$\nIII. - $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234500 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234500 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360150 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234503 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186234503 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. - $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. - ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234503 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 false] [17592186234503 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357759 1081 "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.764\\,9" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357759 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357759 1084 #uuid "0036f17f-24d6-5d5e-ac7d-c6bd3639273c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357760 1081 "({t}^{2}-1){e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357760 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357760 1084 #uuid "0047f2c1-1694-5db5-a45f-e4e293a64ff1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186357761