60456 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358832 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360456 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358932 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360456 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360456 1077 "selection" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360456 1079 #edb/ref 17592186358832 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360456 1080 #uuid "fef06df3-e845-5506-88cf-24141d7c0919" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358667 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358798 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359013 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359173 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359548 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1077 "selection" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1079 #edb/ref 17592186359548 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360457 1080 #uuid "fef695bf-af69-5590-8e72-7ce89ea59101" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358235 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358307 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358665 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358685 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359388 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1077 "selection" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1079 #edb/ref 17592186358685 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360458 1080 #uuid "ffdd071c-0cd6-566d-9f12-642b8f803854" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360166 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1069 "q-94535" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1072 #uuid "bfd947bd-5354-520e-ab7d-3fdc8dc64881" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360459 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{y}]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360259 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1069 "q-94586" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1071 "![](images/75/7583a2da0bff7d66a3512c58f67692100af403b61b19a9b52347519b30823342.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed between  the parts of  the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ as shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1072 #uuid "964ac670-5069-5e05-98d6-a63ad271c9c1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360460 1074 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a half-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radiuses $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\rho (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\rho (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[r]_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\, \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\pi .$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360254 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1069 "q-9459" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-20x+6y+51=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1072 #uuid "14e35fbe-ba48-5ee1-b574-caed502d9c9c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360461 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-20x+6y+51 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-20x+6\\cdot 0+51 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+51 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+51 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+51 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+51 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2(x-5)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that $2(x-5)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360264 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1069 "q-94602" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1071 "Find the center of the elliptic cone $7{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=21{z}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1072 #uuid "1a38f092-f79f-5bb1-8542-be84baf6acc6" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360462 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x-0)^{2}+3(y-0)^{2} & =21(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/7}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{1/3} & =\\frac{(z-0)^{2}}{1/21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(0,0,0).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360360 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1069 "q-94603" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1072 #uuid "31a259dc-025a-58e8-9817-a723f9ec881c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360463 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 0+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $y$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360127 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1069 "q-94605" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2},$ find its intercept with the $z$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1072 #uuid "9922d0a8-63a6-5f94-b280-3937a6da1add" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360464 1074 "To find the $z$-intercept of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{9} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 0+0 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $z$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360251 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1069 "q-94612" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{2}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=2}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1072 #uuid "ef92c245-5042-5dba-9942-02e46fcd2132" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360465 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =2+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=2$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2\\cdot 2=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360260 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1069 "q-94615" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{4}[t]\\,|\\,p(0)=1}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1072 #uuid "ec5fca83-ae3c-5f26-9292-3c61fcf8876c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360466 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(0)=g(0)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)+g(0) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(0)=1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(0) & =1\\cdot 2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360328 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1069 "q-94624" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $E$ containing all diagonal $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1072 #uuid "2ffc0d2e-16d3-51e9-8958-0f5807298d55" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360467 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $E,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $E,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360284 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1069 "q-94625" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ 1-a & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1072 #uuid "32515201-cdee-5802-875e-aaeaf04119b1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360468 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360126 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1069 "q-94629" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $F={A\\in {\\text{M}}_{2}(R)\\,|\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A=A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $F$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $F$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1072 #uuid "4c523e99-06d7-537c-99b6-2e21ff3e81b7" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360469 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $F,$ then ${A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2}.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & ={A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]({A}_{1}+{A}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $F,$ then $A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A.$ For $k\\in R,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kA)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & =k\\cdot A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}](kA).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $kA$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $F$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360114 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1069 "q-94631" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1072 #uuid "10fb8ec2-9c2e-55ae-85b0-700b4fea54e4" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360470 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $H$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360438 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1069 "q-94632" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under function addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1072 #uuid "6847dae1-289b-5050-b4ae-95ca49b57e85" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360471 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360325 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1069 "q-94633" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Q$ is closed under function addition\n- $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1072 #uuid "9ee30622-b701-5da4-9a44-4e5ce669fd87" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360472 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $Q$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360431 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1069 "q-94635" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under function addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1072 #uuid "0f62d508-5cf6-5993-b139-949d11db546e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360473 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360083 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1069 "q-94642" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-2,2]\\to R$ and its subset $E$ containing all even functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under function addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1072 #uuid "44096d11-ac2c-5302-8668-0431e5543e9f" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360474 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in E,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(-x) & =f(-x)+g(-x) \\\\  & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =(f+g)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in E$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(-x) & =kf(-x) \\\\  & =kf(x) \\\\  & =(kf)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360038 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1069 "q-94649" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1071 "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1072 #uuid "7ff3e8b0-9d12-58ec-aef1-61a782559716" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360475 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360151 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1069 "q-9466" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1072 #uuid "9e233b13-1e84-5df5-9c65-e7c695e9cd04" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360476 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ 2+(y-1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =1\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,1-2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,1+2\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360376 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1069 "q-94661" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1071 "Let $F$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $F.$\n- For the zero polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F$ such that $p(x)+q(x)\\ne q(x)+p(x).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1072 #uuid "998609c4-bfb7-5e7d-ad89-b03b40316ca2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360477 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot x+0.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F,$ we have that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of polynomials is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360129 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1069 "q-9468" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1071 "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1072 #uuid "81870e2c-d547-5f03-aff7-b63f30c1daff" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360478 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}+4y]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y+2)^{2}-4]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}-24+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $6(y+2)^{2}=-4.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360070 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1069 "q-94686" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1071 "Let $E$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2}+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$\n- For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1072 #uuid "86d246c9-ffba-5c6c-b83e-58058d87e01e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360479 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the constant polynomial $1$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n1=0\\cdot {x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nHence, $1\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n1+p(x)=1+{x}^{2}+1={x}^{2}+2\\ne p(x).\n$$\n\nThe zero-polynomial $0$ is the unique polynomial such that for any $p(x)\\in E$ we have $0+p(x)=p(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in E,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360194 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1069 "q-94698" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1071 "Find the center of the elliptic cone $9(x+3)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2}={z}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1072 #uuid "b8a937fd-a8d7-55e4-aa58-5b69cf851776" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360480 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x+3)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{1/9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/4} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{1/9}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/4} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(-3,-2,0).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360245 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1069 "q-94700" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1071 "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1072 #uuid "d30a6a74-1882-58de-a0e8-caf74485928f" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360481 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1([\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360232 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1069 "q-94711" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))=2p(x)-2q(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1072 #uuid "0778fc80-bc7a-5da8-b455-9670cb2d5afb" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360482 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360228 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1069 "q-94720" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1071 "![](images/51/514a3cd39f24a7b2477e6214964d69cd743a26645eba7f4b797af832b565fa93.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Find the mass of the object." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1072 #uuid "7ff989f3-597b-5788-b460-bc25edc4c69a" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360483 1074 "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/7f/7f3b2717946956ade07c252413130a730070e370cab85c73d866fa9506604106.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\frac{26}{3}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360299 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1069 "q-94723" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1072 #uuid "03514ad7-d8b6-5b82-8d41-07ae43d6027c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360484 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360334 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1069 "q-94731" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1072 #uuid "96ce050e-6292-5b2a-b7c7-0c1f7f51192d" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360485 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360276 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1069 "q-94734" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1072 #uuid "8c28bdf3-28d7-5fd9-bd3d-116dde5b3b96" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360486 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360407 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1069 "q-94741" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of functions of the form $p(x)=ax,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).$\n- Multiplication by $1$ does not change elements in $U.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1072 #uuid "aa6bb928-4008-5011-93e0-eb9181dde1e2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360487 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360418 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1069 "q-94744" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1071 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1072 #uuid "08e6755d-cc77-5492-aef8-539543b12cc4" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360488 1074 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360158 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1069 "q-94747" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y},$ where $x={e}^{st}$ and $y={e}^{-st}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1072 #uuid "2df77054-e089-51e6-a47e-2d85379912a1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360489 1074 "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360020 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1069 "q-94749" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st).$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1072 #uuid "878a1123-ef9a-5a02-91a8-3dc4077906e0" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360490 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st))(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s{cos}^{2}⁡(st)-s{sin}^{2}⁡(st) \\\\  & =s({cos}^{2}⁡(st)-{sin}^{2}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360055 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1069 "q-94750" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1071 "Find the mass of a plastic object which has the form of a lower half-disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1072 #uuid "d9beb23a-5e95-5e53-9cdc-3f535c894b7a" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360491 1074 "The mass of a lower half-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/37/379073190f15a5e80c696b8f5bb77132dd515ca2493b8f0407354bbac923bb91.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (64-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}]_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=32\\pi .$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360089 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1069 "q-94753" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1071 "Find the mass of an ice object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the fourth quadrant, of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1072 #uuid "75756abe-0233-5c07-9663-7f4aeb820070" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360492 1074 "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360493 1068 #edb/ref 175921