g entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are  the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the the first equation to get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-3{x}_{3}}{-2{x}_{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360308 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1069 "q-94776" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent and ${v}_{1}=2{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}+{v}_{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1072 #uuid "0b4abe9a-af7a-5741-a378-7c07564b4cd7" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360499 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, solving equation ${v}_{1}=2{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}+{v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas only zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360161 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1069 "q-94779" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1072 #uuid "cbf9c53e-84bd-5e5c-95e0-1c3b39f57ad9" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360500 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}{v}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360432 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1069 "q-94781" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{3}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${2{v}_{1},{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1072 #uuid "db8c1e1e-2555-50dd-bb83-7ef5667f1f43" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360501 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+1\\cdot ({v}_{3}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{3}-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{3}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+(3{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1},{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false.  If ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360049 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1069 "q-94784" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1071 "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1072 #uuid "e5c485dc-7f99-51a9-8d40-81cda196a56c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360502 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=2t-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2} & =(2t-8)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-32t+64 \\\\ 32t & =64 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=2 \\\\ z=2(2)-8=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,2,-4).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360162 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1069 "q-94785" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1071 "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{3}=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1072 #uuid "2d0776c2-71d8-59cf-b1b3-9e63eb34ada8" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360503 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=3t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 2(3t)^{2}+5(t-1)^{2} & =2(3t)^{2} \\\\ 5(t-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3(1)=3 \\\\ y=1-1=0 \\\\ z=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,0,3).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360294 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1069 "q-94787" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1072 #uuid "1a997acb-de91-5ebd-a6cc-103226757603" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360504 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive (${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360160 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1069 "q-94795" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1072 #uuid "13d1d5cf-031d-5ebf-8e4c-d550f344afdd" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360505 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360417 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1069 "q-94797" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1072 #uuid "81d008a7-fd35-5fff-9f9d-fc0e8e60bfec" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360506 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 