\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360508 1072 #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360508 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360359 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1069 "q-94805" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1072 #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360509 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360174 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1069 "q-94806" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1072 #uuid "b0bcde67-e6f1-5859-87e0-ff664264d2ea" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360510 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360033 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1069 "q-94807" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1071 "Let $K$ be the set of all $2\\times 1$ matrices $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ such that $x,y\\in R$ and $x+y=1.$  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1072 #uuid "ec097acf-f56e-507d-aaff-ec7126bba2f2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360511 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because **the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K.$** *  \n\nIndeed, adding the entries of the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix yields $0+0=0\\ne 1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- addition of matrices is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- addition and scalar multiplication of matrices  satisfy the distributive laws. $✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot A=A$ for all $A\\in K.$ $\\,✓$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360327 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1069 "q-94809" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1071 "Let $J$ be the set of purely imaginary complex numbers, that is, complex numbers of the form  $ai,$ where  $a$ is a nonzero real number. Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$J$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of complex numbers and scalar multiplication of a complex number by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1072 #uuid "1f59dac0-6b4c-534f-a144-e2ffbf4408ef" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360512 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$J$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of complex numbers and scalar multiplication of a complex number by a real number) because **multiplication of $z\\in J$ by real scalars is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =0$ and $z=i,$ then $\\alpha \\cdot z=0\\cdot i=0\\notin J.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $J$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition  in $J$ is commutative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $J$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- If  $z\\in J$ then $z=ai$ for some $a\\in R.$ Hence, $-z=(-a)i\\in J$   since $-a\\in R.$ $\\,✓$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360351 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1069 "q-94811" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=3x-2y+z,$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}-{t}^{2}$ and $z=s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1072 #uuid "4381d9a9-c913-5a57-ad60-d264f0f8dbf1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360513 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2t)+(1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360422 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1069 "q-94812" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ where $x=2,$ $y=s{e}^{t}$ and $z=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1072 #uuid "8b931e70-9d8d-58d8-83d5-a3ea5f463c4f" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360514 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=xz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2s{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =s{e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(ts{e}^{t+s})\\cdot 0+(2t{e}^{s})\\cdot s{e}^{t}+(2s{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{s} \\\\  & =2ts{e}^{t+s}+2s{e}^{t+s} \\\\  & =2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360343 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1069 "q-94813" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z},$ where $x=s-t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z=s+t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1072 #uuid "e0e34c19-8056-5c3e-8be3-3398dc28d15e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360515 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+y+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x+y+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s-t)+{t}^{2}+(s+t)}={e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot (-1)+({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t+({e}^{2s+{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}+2t{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}+{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t{e}^{2s+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360452 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1069 "q-94815" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},$ where $x=t+2s,$ $y=3t-s$ and $z=st.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1072 #uuid "e1a4443a-d602-5476-a3eb-e3b0f2ea7373" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360516 1074 "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(t+2s)=2t+4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(3t-s)=6t-2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2t+4s)\\cdot 2+(6t-2s)\\cdot (-1)+(2st)\\cdot t \\\\  & =4t+8s-6t+2s+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =10s-2t+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2(5s-t+s{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360350 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1069 "q-94816" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2{e}^{y}+4z,$ where $x=t{s}^{2},$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})$ and $z={s}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1072 #uuid "e95a8899-bb64-5745-b230-495ea8deeef9" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360517 1074 "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})}=-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2ts, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}})\\cdot 2ts+(-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}))\\cdot (\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}})+(4)\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-4s+8s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}+4s \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360365 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1069 "q-94828" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{7}^{2})$ with $P(X<6)=0.6736,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1072 #uuid "72ca84bf-4680-572e-83bf-734897119621" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360518 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6)=0.6736\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 6)=0.6736\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6736 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ \\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.45)=0.6736\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)=0.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & =0.45 \\\\ 6-\\mu  & =3.15 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360369 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1069 "q-94842" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- ${t}^{2}+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${t,{t}^{2}}$\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1072 #uuid "ac8aaff6-9636-552f-aae2-acac5b76a9ac" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360519 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of\n\n$$\n{t,{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nresult in polynomials without the constant term.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, the polynomial ${t}^{2}+1$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set  ${t,{t}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360019 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1069 "q-94847" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $3{t}^{2}-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1072 #uuid "d8792da5-8116-548d-a9e9-a07944635528" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360520 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}-t=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot t+3\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360241 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1069 "q-94848" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- ${t}^{3}-3t+8$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1072 #uuid "b873442b-9d2d-5d41-a7b6-d67882fe4b7f" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360521 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{4}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3t+8=8\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+1\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any polynomial in ${R}_{3}[t],$ we get \n\n$$\na{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+d=d\\cdot 1+c\\cdot t+b\\cdot {t}^{2}+a\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360340 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1069 "q-94850" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1072 #uuid "bf9e33e0-00b4-5d7d-a1a7-55573563c2a8" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360522 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360097 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1069 "q-94856" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-3.5,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-2)=0.7257,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1072 #uuid "bb76f9f2-cc25-5bf7-82e1-e8ee2cf74fb2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360523 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-2)=0.7257\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -2)=0.7257\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -2) & =0.7257 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-2+3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.7257 \\\\ \\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7257)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.60)=0.7257\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7257)=0.60.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & =0.60 \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2.5}^{2}=6.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360433 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1069 "q-94857" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1.8,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 4)=0.2119,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1072 #uuid "4c334f93-902b-5abd-92eb-8183b7608f95" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360524 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X\\ge 4) \\\\  & =1-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-1.8}{\\sigma }) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{2.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.80)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.80.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.2}{\\sigma } & =0.80 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2.2}{0.80} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2.75}^{2}=7.56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360287 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1069 "q-94859" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1072 #uuid "0064f075-2267-5e41-8556-443f519b3150" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360525 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360134 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [