{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ a & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360022 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1069 "q-94864" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1072 #uuid "6ffbe58b-5d9d-5a11-952b-a458019ed538" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360527 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360147 13194139533433 1759218