equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360211 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1069 "q-94873" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{-t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1072 #uuid "97fd3ba8-8ab5-5d07-adaa-98357dd8d4e1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360530 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{-t}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j)\\cdot ((-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})+2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360283 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1069 "q-94878" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ and $r(t)=2\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1072 #uuid "f3d7bbdd-6122-519c-8393-02c2402f7ab7" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360531 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=-2\\sqrt{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-(-2\\sqrt{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})i) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))] & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360412 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1069 "q-94884" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1072 #uuid "8defdc8d-5773-5ad6-96bf-f8efe589fed9" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360532 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360077 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1069 "q-94887" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1072 #uuid "53f0d6f9-0bd0-5fbc-870f-a5ea8fa6cdba" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360533 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360339 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1069 "q-94888" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1072 #uuid "102aa53b-1989-56be-b06a-9a6f6770770c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360534 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360032 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1069 "q-94891" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1072 #uuid "530f5767-4f31-5ef7-a6dd-bdeac420a724" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360535 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360388 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1069 "q-94898" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1072 #uuid "82746228-1e72-5c4c-b5c5-f693d7e57c1b" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360536 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360041 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1069 "q-94908" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1072 #uuid "b7cf5716-597c-5d80-9235-85db49003378" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360537 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360447 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1069 "q-94937" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1072 #uuid "947236b0-fb64-5ba6-b188-fe0713019a58" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360538 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A1\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A4\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360067 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1069 "q-94943" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $V$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w}$ is linearly dependent\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1072 #uuid "592ff49d-bbdc-5145-a575-8c4e0989eb45" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360539 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since the vector ${b}_{3}$ can't be written as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}}.$ But then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(-{x}_{2}){b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360143 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1069 "q-94953" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=(z-2)^{2}$ and the plane $2z-y-6=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1072 #uuid "171b5677-14ac-5663-98ab-0712ade09c11" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360540 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-y-6=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{y}{2}+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={(\\frac{y}{2}+3-2)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={(\\frac{y}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+y+1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}-4y+4}{4}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a circle of radius $2\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360209 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1069 "q-94999" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1071 "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1072 #uuid "4970bf30-0b07-5dcd-b3de-478f21daf6db" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360541 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360085 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1069 "q-95000" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=8?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1072 #uuid "6d906a6e-3108-5a25-a6bd-473a36717188" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360542 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360175 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1069 "q-95003" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1071 "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1072 #uuid "e3bc8d8b-3247-5773-8b80-0a797bc2bf9f" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360543 1074 "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =1\\cdot (1-t+2{t}^{2})+3\\cdot (t-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-t+2{t}^{2}+3t-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360068 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1069 "q-95016" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1072 #uuid "c16597f8-df63-5091-b211-391b9685c9c5" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360544 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+6\\cdot 1 & =-2 \\\\ 4{x}_{1} & =-8 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2+1=-1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360302 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360545 1069 "q-95021" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13