 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360142 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1069 "q-95109" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1071 "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1072 #uuid "b3f6701f-c993-5ff2-926d-7219617af2de" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360569 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360409 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1069 "q-95112" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1072 #uuid "5bc0127e-5521-50db-8fdb-586719893922" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360570 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y