{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{676}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{676}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 130 \\\\ 130 & 650\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{26} & \\frac{5}{26} \\\\ \\frac{5}{26} & \\frac{25}{26}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{T}\\ne C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360059 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1069 "q-95365" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1071 "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{11}+{p}_{23}?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1072 #uuid "b26db0ef-f872-54f3-a0bb-f12c7e09ce6e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360605 1074 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}{a}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={17}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{11}+{p}_{23}=8+4=12.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360309 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1069 "q-95386" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}^{150}$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{150}(R),$ the vector space of all $150\\times 150$ matrices over $R$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1072 #uuid "e531c342-c218-5c9e-86de-28e412cdeaaa" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360606 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}^{150}$ and ${\\text{M}}_{150}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{150})=150\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{150}(R))={150}^{2}=22\\,500,\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360403 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1069 "q-95462" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic cone $(x-1)^{2}+7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2}\\,?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1072 #uuid "8d65c16f-ccd9-5975-b9f2-fd4e5517583b" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360607 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+7(0)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}z & =\\pm (x-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two lines in the $xz$-plane with equations $z=\\pm 4(x-1).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360073 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1069 "q-95490" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1071 "$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1072 #uuid "2f09ab51-13cc-54bd-96f6-b2614a6f4bcc" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360608 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}).\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360012 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360609 1069 "q-95493" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360609 1071 "Given that $y\\ge 0,$ which of the following is 