{4x}={({e}^{2x})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u={e}^{2x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{4x}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{2x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360048 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1069 "q-95738" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1072 #uuid "c3701771-d8dd-50dd-b351-d564f03aacb1" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360634 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{