t{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{7})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360051 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1069 "q-95858" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1071 "$\\int \\frac{12}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1072 #uuid "7533afb6-a59a-5b21-b238-07d91af9b37a" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360645 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\int \\frac{1}{1-(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|4x|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360382 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1069 "q-95861" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1072 #uuid "c718c7b2-75af-5b0a-853d-a82f2bead77e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360646 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|3x|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360112 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1069 "q-95862" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1072 #uuid "8993f120-1289-56c1-a375-830d569d1ba5" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360647 1074 "We proceed to find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{12}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360455 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1069 "q-95863" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1071 "$\\int \\frac{5}{9-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1072 #uuid "a0231fda-6ee5-552f-8769-761c14f66200" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360648 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{9-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{9-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{3}|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360267 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1069 "q-95864" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1071 "$\\int \\frac{12}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1072 #uuid "975f8175-dbf1-5c5c-b8c4-c5b39a19dd5e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360649 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{12}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{4}|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360010 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1069 "q-95865" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1071 "$\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1072 #uuid "d6db16a2-5b4f-5aa0-9944-c1d3b5889da6" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360650 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360319 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1069 "q-95866" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1072 #uuid "17904605-6647-5e68-beba-706add078272" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360651 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{2}|<1.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360337 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1069 "q-95883" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1072 #uuid "a52baa3b-5786-5a03-8f5d-9fe03d10b250" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360652 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡