embles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360425 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1069 "q-95900" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1072 #uuid "3d70b2c7-140b-5f01-a1ce-8684ee01d8ec" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360655 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\in