n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.8119\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.0537\\,$ | $\\,2.1701\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ |" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360735 1072 #uuid "a7c8b775-7560-5ac5-9240-67c2b7733134" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360735 1074 "From the table, notice that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le 1.9600)=0.975.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\ge 1.9600)=1-0.975=0.025.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the standard normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.9600)=0.025.\n$$\n\n![](images/8a/8a3513b66e17745585f5f49ea3cb1b1c7241874159e3cfaca0e5f644d0b7a3c2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.9600) & =0.025 \\\\ P(Z\\le -1.9600) & =0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ is $-1.9600.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360413 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1069 "q-96964" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1072 #uuid "8b3377bc-9e06-5921-a3ff-9b20ca38677d" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360736 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡6x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360093 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1069 "q-96968" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1072 #uuid "9f25f21f-3ef3-5c7e-ab18-78e268c9a510" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360737 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1-\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360238 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1069 "q-96974" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1071 "$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+az \\\\ bx-3z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ shown above. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1072 #uuid "53c0832f-dc15-5565-a318-0725a0cdfe01" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360738 1074 "Given the vector $\\,[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7x+2y-5z \\\\ 3x-3z\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+(-5)z \\\\ 3x-3z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=(-5)+3=-2.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360416 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360739 1069 "q-96977" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360739 1071 "$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}ay+3z-x \\\\ bz-2x+cy\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ 