of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{0}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{0})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-2}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-2)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-2})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{0})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-2})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360196 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1069 "q-96981" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1072 #uuid "f6272326-663e-5a39-9245-c50c7e3c9d7c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360741 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 9 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-4}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{2}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})+\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360071 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1069 "q-96984" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1072 #uuid "3e48487b-d424-5599-a00b-562db69694be" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360742 1074 "Since the matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries of the main diagonal. Therefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{3}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{3})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{3})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360222 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1069 "q-96988" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1072 #uuid "765ad5be-eea7-5159-b982-461fb59d4587" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360743 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360244 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1069 "q-96990" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1072 #uuid "6a63b835-35e3-55e6-8b41-bbb1ab5d0792" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360744 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360109 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360745 1069 "q-97000" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360745 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1\\end{al