\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360268 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1069 "q-97078" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1072 #uuid "2667e6b9-8784-550d-a849-0ee298d78d90" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360757 1074 "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360157 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1069 "q-97111" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1072 #uuid "6581e4b5-aa8f-5ae3-b2d6-35a014836ab5" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360758 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360456 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1069 "q-97112" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1072 #uuid "2230942d-3fef-5be3-b404-1b871db30c25" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360759 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360057 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360760 1069 "q-97119" 1319