y if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨4a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{(1-2\\text{i})}-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4(1+2\\text{i})-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+8\\text{i}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360170 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1069 "q-97215" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1072 #uuid "589fd79e-dcf6-5066-9979-9da6a025b573" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360777 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360272 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1069 "q-97229" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨ia,ia⟩=⟨a,a⟩$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1072 #uuid "ea5645c8-5c4c-53e6-a126-c5a12be2f712" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360778 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $