true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360321 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1069 "q-97278" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1072 #uuid "9c1ef303-e889-5fc1-8e48-5dba05ad5f94" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360782 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360094 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360783 1069 "q-97282" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360783 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C