d}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360213 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1069 "q-97283" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1072 #uuid "b8a45af8-9088-5825-b97b-d38a2bf5a12a" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360784 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transforma