pute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 21 & -15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360173 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1069 "q-97288" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1072 #uuid "cae22cc3-a67e-53bb-bb36-eebf3af162ea" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360785 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360191 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1069 "q-97289" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1072 #uuid "f6350e51-d7f7-5f85-8d67-4cf2337fbb2c" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360786 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=2(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=2(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=2(1)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69065d8e064b0fd679f059925f5445dcb2c7d037f180d3a144ee527ea408e8db.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360088 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1069 "q-97291" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1072 #uuid "7fed752d-147f-589f-b531-b8f9d3875529" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360787 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=(-2)^{2}=4>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=(1)^{2}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c6e2e486ae770bc99ef9fe41f02d224de65119ade910d303ad3f57f12e8d84b.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360230 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1069 "q-97292" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1072 #uuid "0c547f26-e43a-5839-bf5f-d8b1c5da69a2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360788 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=-{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(1,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/272427db87d74e3d4ae09dc69b7add53e8def9ead184a1ab90afc99fc0a7628e.png)" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360122 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1069 "q-97296" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1072 #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360789 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360104 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1069 "q-97303" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1072 #uuid "8247f7d2-00cd-54b9-9bd1-43c13383de3e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360790 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360165 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1069 "q-97306" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1072 #uuid "0d0792af-c54b-5167-a6cf-da7598a4b23d" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360791 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linea