ue] [17592186360811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360811 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=3-t+{t}^{2}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360811 1072 #uuid "4ddf1899-b7cc-54e8-87be-cd6a728234fc" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360811 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=3-t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(-2)=9,\\,p(0)=3,\\,p(2)=5.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(-2)p(-2)+p(0)p(0)+p(2)p(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(-2)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81+9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{115}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360414 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1069 "q-9743" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1072 #uuid "95917747-a74b-509e-9924-fa8c1433f09e" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360812 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360246 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1069 "q-97436" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t-2{t}^{2}$ and $y(t)=t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1072 #uuid "e0f52dc2-3690-578a-8c74-85a5e7f071e3" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360813 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2{t}^{2})(t+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt+(1-2k){t}^{2}-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(1-2k){t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3} & =0 \\\\ 3(k-1)+2(1-2k) 