33 17592186045585 true] [17592186360822 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360436 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1069 "q-97452" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1071 "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1072 #uuid "a7e7515b-c5f0-52cd-b9cf-1d4546466e79" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360823 1074 "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360393 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1069 "q-97464" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1071 "Given that $X∼B(6,0.6),$ compute $P(X=3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1072 #uuid "96496004-4440-5516-85e1-efe7ac7128a7" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360824 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{6-3} \\\\  & =20(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.2765\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360063 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1069 "q-97468" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1071 "Given that $X∼B(6,0.8),$ compute $P(X=4).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1072 #uuid "af8ce92a-7e33-5de2-bb00-872c0b2c3c50" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360825 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.8),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.8)^{4}(0.2)^{6-4} \\\\  & =15(0.8)^{4}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.2458\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360265 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1069 "q-97469" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1071 "Given that $X∼B(7,0.7),$ compute $P(X=5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1072 #uuid "5ff9e580-91d8-537d-9f13-4dec659314b9" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360826 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5})(0.7)^{5}(0.3)^{7-5} \\\\  & =21(0.7)^{5}(0.3)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.3177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360024 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1069 "q-97477" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1072 #uuid "9ebbc31b-8f74-56d3-8d4f-392a8aeeda85" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360827 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=-xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360423 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1069 "q-97482" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1072 #uuid "4ffe3a0e-6b8b-5f1d-8f45-5dd040381266" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360828 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=9xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360091 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1069 "q-97487" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4x{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1072 #uuid "90e895ee-1be8-55c1-ab2d-a3596627f1b5" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360829 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/96/96c937cf10d45d8870a4a56e257d184dcf2e4caf11312c05df9f385f9c5ee7a8.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360312 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1069 "q-9749" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a series $\\frac{1}{2}+2+8+⋯?$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1072 #uuid "bd243b41-9dcd-5b05-a7a5-352e7ff04f04" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360830 1074 "Note that $\\frac{1}{2}+2+8+⋯$ is the sum of a geometric series since the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nFirst, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $r=4.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1-{4}^{N})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1-{4}^{N})}{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{3}\\cdot ({4}^{N}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({4}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360034 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1069 "q-97495" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+2)(y+2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1072 #uuid "a885d23a-7465-5389-b01f-26298ac26e61" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360831 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+2)(y+2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-2$ or $y=-2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d225d82eee3043380eda76506b28e81aee06d42a232348b91b0d5ceb04f3318.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9eedd3b8d82415e8f3126236f2e3a3b4e326a7072057bbd21406da92a08139.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360304 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1069 "q-97503" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1071 "Given that $X∼B(3,0.3),$ compute $P(X\\ge 2).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1072 #uuid "32727390-885c-5602-b623-b9ec09406443" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360832 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{3-3} \\\\  & =3(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360314 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1069 "q-97510" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1071 "Given that $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(X>4).$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1072 #uuid "a2f49332-b965-5b6e-9513-00e213772e19" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360833 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.2)^{5}(0.8)+1(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{0} \\\\  & =0.0016.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360415 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1069 "q-97511" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1071 "Given $X∼B(8,0.2),$ compute $P(2<X<5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1072 #uuid "57e9b330-0c30-55cf-af85-212601b1e4d2" 13194139533433 17592186045585 true] [17592186360834 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(8,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{8-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<5) & =P(X\\in {3,4}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{8-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{8-4} \\\\  & =56(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{5}+70(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.1927\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139533433 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0036f17f-24d6-5d5e-ac7d-c6bd3639273c" 17592186357759 "answer-0047f2c1-1694-5db5-a45f-e4e293a64ff1" 17592186357760 "answer-005cf5a4-2e